#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "fdf51620-58e9-5886-93e5-02a814b154bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8905" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "ae72af2a-ec7e-569d-89a8-550c8eeabdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8909" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81f78a7c-cd88-56d1-8f8c-3b34e686486a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\tan y\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125889} {:answer/id #uuid "97af915f-c569-501c-b73b-a38405a2a1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}y\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{4} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125890} {:answer/id #uuid "caf0c590-9ab9-59be-9b17-711d20f78e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}y\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{4} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125891} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1abc4-7a51-549e-9304-fd818136c6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}y\\sqrt{1 + \\csc^{2} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125892} {:answer/id #uuid "179f1b15-f627-50ff-b478-43eb452483e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97af915f-c569-501c-b73b-a38405a2a1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}y\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{4} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125890} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e4e8fc-9905-5bd1-873b-5ab1f8e8ddfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c12e390-7cac-5d15-8cb3-07a2b192a6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-104533" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a26fcb7-dd8a-5338-afc8-d3346caf5af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387030} {:answer/id #uuid "a141414b-dd92-556c-8029-8f7e488e8a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387031} {:answer/id #uuid "73c71bf5-78f3-503c-a5c2-9b1777e83a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x+1)^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387032} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2363e5-fed2-505d-a7b3-cfd6f73c84f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-x-1)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387033} {:answer/id #uuid "43f8de21-5132-56dc-b00e-16bcc25ac974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a26fcb7-dd8a-5338-afc8-d3346caf5af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387030} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b847f4-9c39-5384-8695-2e0e2e99e7bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "caf996b3-98c2-50cc-98cb-eb0ffa63955b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109123" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/924444fa2da1709d788d3b3522e86743b46ee1c5a25999bb77731449e621a07d.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=1-(x+1)^{2}$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-2,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-(x+1)^{2})=-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-(x+1)^{2})\\sqrt{1+(-2x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd33f6bc-be76-5811-acf9-eac8fbeec2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387037} {:answer/id #uuid "55435d40-60df-5029-9541-d3418b01a072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387038} {:answer/id #uuid "f18e0084-a327-5799-8f69-0abf48158eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387039} {:answer/id #uuid "53ec25e1-d3f1-5a34-a28e-65a51c59f377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387040} {:answer/id #uuid "48b4df15-19e4-5784-b061-e31b571bf95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48b4df15-19e4-5784-b061-e31b571bf95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387041} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbff233-1b51-5b4b-add4-c165709eaaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "630e6917-1b31-5ed2-909b-732e3490f5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-109124" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=1$ and $x=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=1$ to ${x}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d48d9c0-2fd6-52ed-a63d-b4280c603f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387044} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea1d500-c4f5-5588-9f66-4ed34cdba364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387045} {:answer/id #uuid "809a2df8-f0bb-5c25-b7cb-cdcd7c6e6926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387046} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8ef166-260e-5b93-a31a-1fa7c86fe533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387047} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9bb627-c3d3-5218-9b65-2e009e2d03f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809a2df8-f0bb-5c25-b7cb-cdcd7c6e6926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387046} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac69c48-690b-5dbb-90d9-c7fef8cfbcfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3725a9b-dcb4-5fa2-b792-1fe048d5722b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109126" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/0edb5218c2d1527957053a35e992c7538a8857132a6d48114e16e5f71d3848d6.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb2fc180-a898-5391-a02c-2840c3930a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387051} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d5ff1c-5418-58fd-9cc2-c5fe364627fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{(2-x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387052} {:answer/id #uuid "ac831a82-a090-54d6-9010-ffc5d03e7366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387053} {:answer/id #uuid "df7ee050-ac27-587b-8a1c-e8f6c2330af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387054} {:answer/id #uuid "d80f9add-3302-57c0-ab4c-9a7dd64c9b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d80f9add-3302-57c0-ab4c-9a7dd64c9b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387055} :answer-field/id #uuid "99b52f2f-571f-5ea6-b5a1-79051e19fdf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95f65cb5-fc27-5c03-8504-7e25fce5f434" :question/math-academy-id "q-109143" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2-\\sqrt{x}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3846b18-068f-5d46-b8e9-ae330a812c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387058} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ee6ab-b700-5c7a-8309-f7bc7b0e046a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387059} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0482db-620c-5115-9d37-2e8fd660b699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387060} {:answer/id #uuid "2f29ee2e-c0e3-59ac-9766-f0fd265878bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387061} {:answer/id #uuid "f86696a7-7152-54f4-b6f6-64a05f3e2976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ee6ab-b700-5c7a-8309-f7bc7b0e046a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387059} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b2d52e-608f-56ca-b7c9-86ab9b88ad0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52c76f08-0943-5c3e-9636-b2f7eab362d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109155" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/22bb16a874005f09d5cbad055f149f07e2139f559a2a04368c32696567714b26.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $y=0$ and its $y$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edd53f6-dbf6-5462-8d89-178b22fe4c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{2y - y^{3}}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y - y^{3}}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125901} {:answer/id #uuid "ac863c0c-12ac-5af9-989a-512c1472148c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y - y^{3}}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125902} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ff329-cbec-572f-819b-87f004c11751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125903} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ee36fc-7458-5f3c-93ba-3fe804a66343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2y - y^{3})\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125904} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec62c73-022a-53f5-a458-fe05bdd41ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{2}y^{2}\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ff329-cbec-572f-819b-87f004c11751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125903} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b744d30-5cec-5215-9c0e-5a7df0803be6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18821903-024e-5a0d-8496-6fcfa9ff5b2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109179" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=2y-{y}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(2-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-{y}^{3})=2-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(2-3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "922c0fe5-a954-57dd-9c66-c82340b63378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387065} {:answer/id #uuid "b8214ee2-7c44-59c9-b036-88a88600709a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387066} {:answer/id #uuid "d94a6cfb-ed38-5eb5-9db9-c191cb50a173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387067} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc550d-4716-5c87-8ade-1c3b3b9567f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387068} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8c08f0-c24d-5981-89cf-b253b8083cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{y({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60cc550d-4716-5c87-8ade-1c3b3b9567f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387068} :answer-field/id #uuid "93548155-9136-514c-9ff4-2ad626f0b8d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cb738bd-1657-50aa-acee-1f912676f25f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109188" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/9b27f44aa7ef6bef95a88ccfcb6495d17e9457a7452bf602f87dcd5b95ec71dc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercept and the line $y=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a227ddfa-8f31-529a-aced-ec8333463edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387072} {:answer/id #uuid "3f407bcc-8ed3-5d07-8aa0-85b60cd8bcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387073} {:answer/id #uuid "3f79674a-5183-5d7b-9737-fbfcf90030ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (6-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387074} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf3ffbc-d7e5-582c-9ce3-204ddbd465f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387075} {:answer/id #uuid "73d6c78a-807e-566c-8a7b-820218a705df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a227ddfa-8f31-529a-aced-ec8333463edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387072} :answer-field/id #uuid "4306813d-1d6e-5b41-aec2-235f501e995a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "777aa170-0dc9-512e-8f03-a8a705e1c469" :question/math-academy-id "q-109319" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=9-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}u=-2y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3\\pi {\\int }_{9}^{5}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{9}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {[2\\sqrt{u}\\,]}_{5}^{9} \\\\  & =6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8687539-ed01-557c-83f6-141a2ad1f379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387079} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce72f66-28c7-5cea-8e12-eaec17c94469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387080} {:answer/id #uuid "d456a18e-7bbd-5d16-a6bc-92686e035fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387081} {:answer/id #uuid "4d459b3d-ee43-559a-bb76-a63004876263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387082} {:answer/id #uuid "98131914-04d2-536a-b150-2b199d6772ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d456a18e-7bbd-5d16-a6bc-92686e035fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387081} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a46e88b-c202-5eae-9589-8b035ce9dd67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8299664-2eac-54b1-a2d8-b4c54e28a46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126521" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\sqrt{1+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c157c29-07e2-5505-aefe-3127849fc32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387086} {:answer/id #uuid "c14f66cb-598d-53b3-a7d9-7a82afc56072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387087} {:answer/id #uuid "f48ceb38-e9d5-5645-9034-4ddd4d3c1763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387088} {:answer/id #uuid "bdffeeda-8453-5f19-94c8-d471dbddd8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387089} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa65cf1-8862-5e81-9eb0-646b769e8f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c157c29-07e2-5505-aefe-3127849fc32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387086} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb6d9f6-2140-5991-b864-4fea6b588ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d978c2-8b03-5808-9b4a-ab2bf7c73b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-126551" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1204968a7d4eee9f299b432c21441f670c7ebfab0629da6ef66a7960033a9c1b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x={y}^{2}-2y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}-2y)=2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+{(2y-2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c828721-be3c-5849-8600-f8b2e58afe67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387093} {:answer/id #uuid "dc98e8cd-9d57-5de2-89b9-78853d7a23cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387094} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe596ad-226e-55fa-a4f4-39c8f3e379f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387095} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d3ed15-fc6a-558c-b1f1-13345a642e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (3\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387096} {:answer/id #uuid "c4028995-1484-5909-9581-c2d66f900e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc98e8cd-9d57-5de2-89b9-78853d7a23cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387094} :answer-field/id #uuid "09769ad5-a9f0-5417-8bf1-c063e61e33a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89554c02-d315-5a8d-8464-ca9cd53eae70" :question/math-academy-id "q-126589" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1)=y\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u=y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\frac{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753d94e0-a2a2-549f-8dc0-d6b142b11e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "753d94e0-a2a2-549f-8dc0-d6b142b11e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387100} :answer-field/id #uuid "83bd1d8e-494a-50ce-a109-f8a42a88b9d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2419aff-382b-524d-8eee-9f4042d5d08e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353403" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{3x+1}$ that lies between the lines $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3x+1})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{12x+13}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{(12x+13)^{3/2}}{18}\\,{|}_{-1/3}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}({25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89fec85d-f8a0-5718-825e-7f130d1db9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89fec85d-f8a0-5718-825e-7f130d1db9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387103} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7036810-ada4-5374-a85e-4c88fa0fa505" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45434f1e-aeeb-591d-8147-87710b9c595b" :question/math-academy-id "q-353405" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2x+1$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)=2\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}((1+1)-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd5631d-1ba6-5ff0-913d-bd4ab5adf0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{149\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5631d-1ba6-5ff0-913d-bd4ab5adf0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{149\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387106} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1e728d-36c5-5ec0-987c-1723f9717512" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83dd7e58-e9f5-5d83-b520-e7682449df98" :question/math-academy-id "q-353411" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2})=2\\sqrt{y}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1+4y\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}y,\\,y=\\frac{u-1}{4},\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{u-1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {9}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {9}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 243-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 27)-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{396}{5}+\\frac{4}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{149\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
